<ResourceDictionary xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml">

    <!--
        THE TWO MENUS THIS WINDOW OPENS OVER ITS COLUMNS: the picker under the Columns button, and
        the one under a right click on a column heading. Not one value of its own - ADR-23, the
        same rule as every other file here.

        IT LEFT Themes/Controls.xaml ON 2026-09-05 BECAUSE THE SIZE RATCHET SAID SO, and the seam
        was the one that file already names in its own opening lines: it calls itself the controls
        this window is made of, "the field somebody types into, the box somebody ticks, the chip" -
        and a menu is not one of those. That day put four new styles into it at once, which was the
        picker learning to nest and the heading learning to filter, and it went sixty-six lines past
        a ceiling that may only ever come down.

        ORDER INSIDE THIS FILE IS LOAD BEARING, which is the one thing to know before editing it.
        ColumnGroupItem names ColumnChoiceItem with a StaticResource, and a StaticResource is
        resolved as the dictionary is read - so a style naming one declared BELOW it fails at parse
        time and takes the window down at startup rather than looking wrong.

        AND SO IS THE FILE'S PLACE IN App.xaml: it is merged after Controls.xaml, which is where the
        implicit MenuItem style these all extend is found. Merged first, every BasedOn here would
        resolve to nothing.
    -->

    <Style x:Key="ColumnChoiceItem" TargetType="MenuItem" BasedOn="{StaticResource {x:Type MenuItem}}">
        <Setter Property="Header" Value="{Binding Label}" />
        <Setter Property="IsCheckable" Value="True" />
        <Setter Property="IsChecked" Value="{Binding IsShown, Mode=TwoWay}" />
        <Setter Property="IsEnabled" Value="{Binding MayHide}" />
        <Setter Property="StaysOpenOnClick" Value="True" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        ONE GROUP OF COLUMNS, AS A SUBMENU - 2026-09-05, owner's decision. Until that day this was
        an inert heading in a flat list of thirty-two items, and the menu is capped at 420 units:
        counted from outside with it open, THIRTEEN ITEMS WERE ON SCREEN AND NINETEEN BELOW THE
        FOLD, starting at Publisher. Two whole groups could not be reached without scrolling a menu
        nobody scrolls. The measurement and the arithmetic against the alternative - raising the
        ceiling gives a menu taller than the screen - are at ColumnGroup.

        AFTER ColumnChoiceItem RATHER THAN BEFORE IT, and that is load bearing rather than tidy: a
        StaticResource is resolved as the dictionary is read, so a style naming one declared below
        it fails at parse time and takes the window down at startup.

        A submenu rather than a GroupStyle, which is the repair described in ColumnEntryStyles:
        grouping a menu puts a GroupItem between it and its items and takes the whole lot out of
        the automation tree. A MenuItem holding MenuItems has nothing in between.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="ColumnGroupItem" TargetType="MenuItem" BasedOn="{StaticResource {x:Type MenuItem}}">
        <Setter Property="Header" Value="{Binding Label}" />
        <Setter Property="ItemsSource" Value="{Binding Choices}" />
        <Setter Property="ItemContainerStyle" Value="{StaticResource ColumnChoiceItem}" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        ONE VALUE A COLUMN CAN BE NARROWED TO, under a right click on its heading - 2026-09-05, the
        owner's ask. Bound to a FilterChip, which is the same object the row of chips above the list
        is made of: it holds no state, reads whether it is on out of the query text, and writes a
        member somebody can read. So this tick and that chip are one mechanism reached two ways.

        THE TOOLTIP IS THE MEMBER ITSELF - status:running - which is the `A5` promise: seeing what
        will be written before clicking is half of it, and watching it arrive in the box is the
        other half. The chips have carried the same tooltip since they were built.

        StaysOpenOnClick, because narrowing to running AND stopped is one errand over two clicks.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="ColumnValueItem" TargetType="MenuItem" BasedOn="{StaticResource {x:Type MenuItem}}">
        <Setter Property="Header" Value="{Binding Label}" />
        <Setter Property="ToolTip" Value="{Binding Member}" />
        <Setter Property="IsCheckable" Value="True" />

        <!--
            IsOn AND NOT IsShown, which is what a chip calls this and what the column choices in
            the picker two styles up call theirs. The wrong one of those two binds perfectly, fails
            at run time only, and reports nothing anywhere - the tick would simply never light.
        -->
        <Setter Property="IsChecked" Value="{Binding IsOn, Mode=TwoWay}" />
        <Setter Property="StaysOpenOnClick" Value="True" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        THE LAST ITEM OF THAT MENU, AND ON FIVE COLUMNS THE ONLY ONE. Those five have no field in
        the query language at all, so there are no values to offer - and a menu that refused to open
        on them would teach that the gesture is unreliable, which is the reasoning written out at
        ColumnMenu.

        DISABLED RATHER THAN ABSENT WHEN IT CANNOT BE DONE, which is the picker's own decision about
        the last column showing: a person is told before acting rather than after.

        AND ShowOnDisabled, WITHOUT WHICH THE REASON EXISTS EVERYWHERE EXCEPT ON THE SCREEN. WPF
        shows no tooltip at all on a disabled control unless this says otherwise - trap 15 of
        `docs/10`, paid for once already. The tooltip is set by the trigger rather than always, or
        an item that CAN be clicked would explain why it cannot.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="ColumnHideItem" TargetType="MenuItem" BasedOn="{StaticResource {x:Type MenuItem}}">
        <Setter Property="Header" Value="{Binding HideLabel}" />
        <Setter Property="IsEnabled" Value="{Binding MayHide}" />
        <Setter Property="ToolTipService.ShowOnDisabled" Value="True" />
        <Style.Triggers>
            <Trigger Property="IsEnabled" Value="False">
                <Setter Property="ToolTip" Value="{Binding HideRefused}" />
            </Trigger>
        </Style.Triggers>
    </Style>

    <!--
        THE WAY BACK FROM A LAYOUT SOMEBODY NO LONGER WANTS, 2026-08-25. What is chosen above is
        written to disk and comes back on the next start, so without this item the only road back
        to the six columns this window opens with is turning the others off one at a time.

        NO "TURN THEM ALL ON" BESIDE IT, AND THE REASON IS MEASURED RATHER THAN TIDY. Turning on a
        signature column is what sends the window to open eight hundred files - 8.86-9.42 s of
        processor against 1.39-1.42 s without it, over about 810 entries. One item that switches
        every column on is one item that stops the window for ten seconds.

        THAT SENTENCE WAS NOT TRUE WHEN IT WAS WRITTEN AND BECAME TRUE ON 2026-09-05, which is
        worth more than quietly correcting it. Turning a column on reached exactly one listener,
        the one that writes the layout file - so it cost nothing, read nothing, and the column sat
        at "unknown" on every row. The measurement quoted above is real and belonged to the query
        path. Nothing had ever measured the column path because there was nothing there to
        measure, and prose has no guard - see Column.Needs for the whole account.

        StaysOpenOnClick is deliberately NOT here, unlike the choices above. Ticking four columns is
        one errand spread over four clicks, and this is one errand finished in one - so the menu
        closing is what says it happened.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="ColumnResetItem" TargetType="MenuItem" BasedOn="{StaticResource {x:Type MenuItem}}">
        <Setter Property="Header" Value="{Binding Label}" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        THE THIRD MENU, 2026-09-15: THE ONE ON A ROW, which until that day was written out in
        MainWindow.xaml with a Click on every item. It is a list in RowMenu.cs now - the markup stood
        on the size ratchet's ceiling and "Show details" needed two lines - and every item wears
        this, reading its words off the entry it carries as data.

        THE KEY BESIDE THE LABEL IS THE WHOLE POINT OF THE NEW ITEM. Enter has opened the panel about
        an entry since 2026-08-13 and nothing on screen said so, which `docs/03` part 5 calls a hole
        in discoverability. InputGestureText is where a menu writes its key, the library's template
        draws it - WpfUiContractGuards asks - and it is TEXT rather than a KeyGesture on purpose,
        because the press itself is decided in Shortcuts.cs and a gesture declared here too would be
        the same key in two places. Items with no key bind an empty string, which the template draws
        as nothing.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="RowMenuItem" TargetType="MenuItem" BasedOn="{StaticResource {x:Type MenuItem}}">
        <Setter Property="Header" Value="{Binding Label}" />
        <Setter Property="InputGestureText" Value="{Binding Gesture}" />
    </Style>
</ResourceDictionary>
